1. One task, estimated at one hour

   takes 1.00 h   chance  60.0%
   takes 1.50 h   chance  30.0%
   takes 3.00 h   chance  10.0%

   Most likely time: 1 hour, the estimate. Average time: 1.35 hours.
   The estimate is the most common outcome and still 35% short of the
   average, because the misses are all on one side.

2. A day planned to capacity: n one-hour tasks in n hours

   tasks   planned   average needed   chance the day fits
       1       1 h         1.35 h       60.0%
       2       2 h         2.70 h       36.0%
       3       3 h         4.05 h       21.6%
       4       4 h         5.40 h       13.0%
       5       5 h         6.75 h        7.8%
       6       6 h         8.10 h        4.7%

   Every task is estimated honestly, and a five-task day fits one time
   in thirteen. The day fits only if every task hits its estimate.

3. The buffer hour: five tasks, with b spare hours in the plan

   buffer   chance the day fits
      0 h     7.8%
      1 h    46.7%
      2 h    65.3%
      3 h    88.2%

   One reserved hour turns a day that fits one time in thirteen into one
   that fits nearly half the time. A plan that holds most days needs more
   slack than feels reasonable, or fewer tasks. When nothing goes wrong,
   the book says, enjoy the unplanned 'you time'.

4. Why the errors do not cancel: skewed against symmetric

   The same five tasks, if a task were as likely to finish half an hour
   early as half an hour late (x 1/2, 1, 3/2 with chances 2/10, 6/10, 2/10):

                      average day   fits in 5 h   fits in 6 h
   symmetric errors       5.00 h     64.0%         96.4%
   skewed errors          6.75 h      7.8%         46.7%

   With symmetric errors an early task pays for a late one, and a plan
   of five hours is right on average. Real tasks cannot run much under
   their estimate, so nothing pays for the late ones.

5. Estimates that improve: keep the ratio actual / estimated

   Suppose you write down, for twenty past tasks, estimated and actual time.
   Twelve took the estimate, six took 1.5 times it, two took 3 times it.
   Average ratio: 1.35. Multiply every new estimate by it:

   five one-hour tasks are planned as 6.75 hours
   chance that fits:  56.4%  (unscaled:   7.8%)

   The ratio is metacognition applied to time: compare what you
   predicted with what happened, and correct the prediction, not the
   feeling. The plan now matches the average day; the buffer hour is
   for the days worse than average.
